## Overview
This question evaluates understanding of inter-thread synchronization, producer-consumer patterns, and low-level data structure design in operating system environments.

## Key Concepts
- Circular buffer with head/tail indices and wraparound handling
- Mutexes and condition variables for blocking operations and wakeups
- Memory visibility guarantees via compiler and hardware memory barriers
- Correctness and performance characteristics with multiple concurrent producers

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
    char data[LOG_SIZE];
    int valid;
} slot_t;

slot_t buffer[N];
int head = 0;
int tail = 0;
int count = 0;

mutex_t mtx;
cond_t not_full;
cond_t not_empty;

void enqueue(const char *record) {
    mutex_lock(&mtx);
    while (count >= N)
        cond_wait(&not_full, &mtx);
    buffer[tail % N] = *(slot_t *)record;
    tail++;
    count++;
    cond_signal(&not_empty);
    mutex_unlock(&mtx);
}

void dequeue(char *record) {
    mutex_lock(&mtx);
    while (count == 0)
        cond_wait(&not_empty, &mtx);
    *(slot_t *)record = buffer[head % N];
    head++;
    count--;
    cond_signal(&not_full);
    mutex_unlock(&mtx);
}
```

## Test Plan
1. **Correctness**: Verify no data loss or corruption with single producer/consumer
2. **Blocking**: Confirm enqueue blocks when buffer is full, dequeue blocks when empty
3. **Throughput**: Measure records/second with 1, 4, 8, and 16 concurrent producers
4. **Stress**: Run sustained test to detect race conditions and memory leaks

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of synchronization primitives and memory ordering in a toy OS context.

## Key Concepts

- Barrier reuse via a generation counter
- Race-free wakeups with a condition variable analogue
- Memory visibility and minimal locking
- Testing for progress and fairness under contention

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct {
  int count;
  int needed;
  int gen;
  pthread_mutex_t m;
  pthread_cond_t cv;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n) {
  b->count = 0;
  b->needed = n;
  b->gen = 0;
  pthread_mutex_init(&b->m, NULL);
  pthread_cond_init(&b->cv, NULL);
}

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b) {
  pthread_mutex_lock(&b->m);
  int g = b->gen;
  if (++b->count == b->needed) {
    b->count = 0;
    b->gen++;
    pthread_cond_broadcast(&b->cv);
  } else {
    while (b->gen == g) {
      pthread_cond_wait(&b->cv, &b->m);
    }
  }
  pthread_mutex_unlock(&b->m);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for dynamic participant counts?
- How would you test for spurious wakeups and ensure progress under contention?

