## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical IPC path design, per-core locality optimization, and safe buffer migration under memory pressure in high-performance systems.

## Key Concepts
- **Per-core allocator**: Each CPU core maintains a local free-list of buffers to minimize cache-line bouncing and lock contention
- **Lock-free CAS synchronization**: Compare-and-swap operations enable atomic updates without global locks
- **Cross-core buffer migration**: Moving buffers between cores to balance load while maintaining locality
- **Quotas and fairness**: Ensuring no single client monopolizes buffer resources

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal sketch (C-like) for acquire_buffer and migrate_buffer
typedef struct buf { 
    int id; 
    int state; 
    struct buf *next; 
} buf_t;

typedef struct core_pool { 
    buf_t *free; 
    atomic_int quota; 
} core_pool_t;

buf_t *acquire_buffer(int core) {
    core_pool_t *p = &pools[core];
    buf_t *b;
    do {
        b = atomic_load(&p->free);
        if (!b) return NULL;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->free, &b, b->next));
    atomic_fetch_sub(&p->quota, 1);
    return b;
}

void migrate_buffer(buf_t *b, int to_core) {
    core_pool_t *p = &pools[to_core];
    buf_t *old_head;
    do {
        old_head = atomic_load(&p->free);
        b->next = old_head;
    } while (!atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&p->free, &old_head, b));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free data structures, memory ordering semantics, and cross-platform memory barrier requirements in a production system context.

## Key Concepts
- MPMC ring buffer with per-slot sequence numbers
- ABA problem mitigation via publish/subscribe pattern
- Architecture-specific memory barrier requirements
- Contention handling and wraparound correctness

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-code for enqueue/dequeue operations
bool enqueue(struct ring *r, void *data) {
    size_t pos = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&r->tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    size_t slot = pos % r->capacity;

    // Wait for slot to be available
    while (r->slots[slot].seq != pos) {
        // Backoff or handle contention
    }

    // Write payload
    r->slots[slot].data = data;

    // Publish with release barrier
    atomic_store_explicit(&r->slots[slot].seq, pos + 1, memory_order_release);
    return true;
}

bool dequeue(struct ring *r, void **data) {
    size_t pos = atomic_load_explicit(&r->head, memory_order_relaxed);
    size_t slot = pos % r->capacity;

    // Check if slot is ready with acquire barrier
    size_t seq = atomic_load_explicit(&r->slots[slot].seq, memory_order_acquire);
    if (seq != pos + 1) return false;

    // Read payload
    *data = r->slots[slot].data;

    // Advance head
    atomic_store_explicit(&r->head, pos + 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return true;
}
```

## Memory Barriers by Architecture
- **x86_64**: `memory_order_release`/`memory_order_acquire` (no additional barriers needed)
- **ARM64**: `dmb ish` for release, `dmb ishld` for acquire operations

