## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free inter-process communication under simple multicore constraints and cache coherence protocols. It distinguishes candidates who know the mechanics of atomics from those who understand visibility and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with atomic indices
- Acquire/release semantics for safe cross-core visibility
- Wrap-around via power-of-two masking
- Contention handling and backoff strategies

## Code Example
```c
#define SIZE 1024  // power of two
#define MASK (SIZE - 1)

_Atomic size_t head = 0;
_Atomic size_t tail = 0;
type_t buffer[SIZE];

void enqueue(type_t value) {
    size_t h = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    buffer[h & MASK] = value;
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
}

type_t dequeue(void) {
    size_t t = atomic_load(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
    type_t value = buffer[t & MASK];
    atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return value;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical lock design for multi-socket systems, balancing scalability and correctness under contention.

## Key Concepts

- MCS locks and queue-based synchronization
- NUMA-aware data locality
- Cache-line padding and false sharing avoidance
- Timeout/backoff to prevent livelock

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo C-like sketch for acquire/release
typedef struct Node { volatile int locked; struct Node *next; } Node;
typedef struct Lock { Node *tail; } MCSLock;
void lock_acquire(MCSLock *L, Node *me) {
  me->next = NULL; me->locked = 1;
  Node *pred = atomic_swap(&L->tail, me);
  if (pred) { pred->next = me; while (me->locked) pause(); }
}
void lock_release(MCSLock *L, Node *me) {
  if (!me->next) { if (atomic_compare_and_swap(&L->tail, me, NULL)) return; while (!me->next) pause(); }
  me->next->locked = 0;
}
```

